name: kata
description: Choose, adapt, or reject slide patterns for an audience's question. Use when turning structured information into a clear slide or improving its visual structure. Works with a slide renderer and optional brand skill; does not plan a whole deck narrative.
---

# Kata

Start with the audience's question and the understanding or decision it supports. Identify the information relationship before choosing a visual structure. A slide may communicate a useful fact without making a recommendation.

## Choose a pattern

| Audience question | Pattern | Read |
| --- | --- | --- |
| How do these options differ on the same criteria? | Aligned comparison | [comparison](references/comparison.md) |
| What makes up this total? | Whole and parts | [composition](references/composition.md) |
| What belongs inside what? | Nested groups | [hierarchy](references/hierarchy.md) |
| What depends on what? | Directed dependencies | [relationship](references/relationship.md) |
| How did this measure change? | Time series | [change-over-time](references/change-over-time.md) |
| What is primary, with context beside it? | Asymmetric composition | [golden cut](references/golden-cut.md) |

Read the relevant reference. Check whether the source supports the relationship and whether the medium, density, and reading context keep it legible. Choose a pattern, adapt it, separate questions into slides, or name a better representation outside this catalogue. If none fits, say so and identify what missing information would change the choice. Do not score patterns or imply one wins apart from the purpose.

## Adapt to the information

Identify the audience question, the units and scope of the supplied data, and a supportable headline. Preserve missing values as unknown; distinguish them from zero. Ask for missing information when it changes the meaning, such as whether categories overlap or what a dependency arrow represents. Otherwise proceed with a stated assumption. Explain the consequential trade-off and retain values, units, scope, uncertainty, and caveats.

Keep labels, units, denominators, dates, uncertainty, and sources that affect interpretation. Do not invent measurements, causal explanations, option weights, or winners. The worked examples use fictional data and are layout specimens, not evidence to reuse in a real presentation.

Each reference includes a message, a layout, an example, a failure condition, and a rationale. The rationales are design judgments. Gestalt vocabulary supplies analogies, not proof that a layout is universally effective. When making empirical psychology claims, find and cite research supporting the particular claim.

## Render with the available tools

Kata owns the information structure and its reading order. The renderer owns slide behavior and output format. The brand skill owns typography, colors, imagery, and other visual identity. Preserve meaningful scales, grouping, and labels when applying a brand. The examples' cream and rust theme is replaceable and is not a Futurice theme.

For Lys, read [the integration note](references/integration.md) and use the available Lys skill or documented HTML contract. For another output format, use its authoring workflow. Kata works without Lys or a brand skill and does not install either. Only claim an integration was tested when the actual output has been inspected.

## Review the slide

Check whether a reader can answer the audience question from the visual without reconstructing the source prose. Check data fidelity, meaningful grouping, and the suggested reading order. Make numeric encodings proportional; do not use decorative size or area to imply magnitude. Inspect at presentation size for clipped text, unreadable labels, and connectors that imply the wrong relationship. Preserve a text equivalent for charts and diagrams, and use labels or shape as well as color.

Review label association, emphasis, and legibility in the rendered result. Keep the explanation of the selected pattern and consequential assumptions with the deliverable when useful.

Deliver the slide in the requested format. Include the chosen pattern and consequential assumptions in the accompanying explanation when useful; keep authoring instructions out of the audience's slide.
# Aligned comparison

**Form:** Several alternatives share the same attributes. **Audience question:** Which trade-offs matter for this decision?

Use a matrix when exact values or mixed units matter. Use aligned bars or dots when the question is primarily about one numeric measure. Avoid a matrix for long narrative descriptions or options evaluated on unrelated criteria. Split a large comparison around the actual decision instead of shrinking everything.

## Structure

Put alternatives in columns and criteria in rows, with units in row labels. Keep identical attributes in the same position. Use consistent precision. Put the decisive criterion near the top when the audience has supplied one. Highlight a recommendation only when the data and decision criteria support it. Missing data remains “Unknown,” not a dash that could mean zero.

## Worked example

Fictional service launch. An operations lead needs a launch route within six weeks and an initial budget of €80k. Costs are one-time estimates; lead times are estimates too.

| Criterion | Build | Buy | Partner |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Lead time, weeks | 12 | 4 | 6 |
| Initial cost, €k | 120 | 60 | 80 |
| Control | Full | Limited | Shared |

**Before:** Three paragraphs, each listing its option's time, cost, and control. The audience must remember values across paragraphs.

**After headline:** “Buy and Partner meet both launch limits”

**After layout:** One matrix with the constraints stated above it. Emphasize the time and cost cells for Buy and Partner, retaining their text. State below: “The remaining trade-off is control.” This does not establish a winner or address lifetime cost.

## Rationale and limits

Design judgment: common alignment makes a criterion easy to scan. Similarity is a useful Gestalt analogy for repeated cell treatment; it does not prove this matrix is best for every audience. Avoid unrelated icons, ratings without defined scales, and weighted totals without agreed weights.

**Check:** All nine values survive the transformation, both constraints remain visible, and no option becomes a recommendation by styling alone.
# Directed dependencies

**Form:** Named entities connected by a defined relation. **Audience question:** What must happen before what?

Use a small directed diagram when prerequisite structure matters. Use a timeline when dates or durations are the question. Use a matrix or several focused views for a dense network. Keep direction and edge meaning explicit; data flow, ownership, influence, and prerequisite are different relations.

## Structure

For prerequisite edges, put prerequisites on the left and dependent work on the right. State that an arrow means “must finish before.” Route connectors so endpoints are unambiguous. Preserve forks and joins. Do not imply that horizontal distance means duration. Label different edge types individually if they must coexist.

## Worked example

Fictional service launch. The launch lead needs to explain readiness prerequisites.

- Research must finish before Design.
- Design must finish before Build.
- Build must finish before Launch.
- Compliance must finish before Launch.

**Before:** A list of these four rules.

**After headline:** “Launch requires both Build and Compliance”

**After layout:** Research, Design, Build, and Launch run left to right. Compliance sits on a separate lower branch with its own arrow into Launch. State the edge meaning and “Spacing does not represent duration.” The final join retains two independent prerequisites.

## Rationale and limits

Design judgment: continuous paths make the prerequisite chain traceable. Good continuation is a Gestalt analogy, not proof of easier comprehension. The lower branch is a separate prerequisite, not a later phase. No durations or slack are supplied, so the diagram cannot identify a critical path or a bottleneck.

**Check:** All four source edges survive; no connector merely passes through a box that could look like another dependency. A cycle should remain explicit and trigger a question about its meaning, not be silently deleted.
# Nested groups

**Form:** A rooted structure in which each child has one parent. **Audience question:** What belongs where?

Use nested regions for membership, a tree for reporting lines or deeper parent-child structure, and an indented list when space is tight. Multiple parents or cross-group dependencies call for a relationship diagram. Do not infer reporting authority from mere grouping.

## Structure

Name the root and the relation being shown. Give siblings the same visual treatment and place children within their parent region. Keep spacing between groups greater than spacing within them. Use equal areas when size has no meaning. Limit visible depth to what the audience needs, with a separate detail slide if required.

## Worked example

Fictional service launch. A new team member needs the workstream map, not a reporting chart.

- Launch programme
  - Experience: Research, Design
  - Delivery: Engineering, Operations

**Before:** One sentence listing the programme, workstreams, and four disciplines.

**After headline:** “Launch work belongs to two workstreams”

**After layout:** A single enclosing region named Launch programme. Inside, two equal regions named Experience and Delivery, each containing its two disciplines. Add “Workstream membership; area does not indicate team size.”

## Rationale and limits

Design judgment: containment expresses membership without a tangle of connectors. Common region is the Gestalt inspiration. Equal region size is a deliberate choice to avoid implying staffing levels. It is not evidence that the workstreams have equal capacity or influence.

**Check:** Each discipline has exactly one parent in the supplied example. Do not silently duplicate a shared team to make the diagram fit.
# Whole and parts

**Form:** Mutually exclusive parts exhaust a known total. **Audience question:** Where does the whole go?

Use a single stacked bar for a broad allocation and direct labels for exact amounts. If precise ranking of similar parts is the main question, use aligned bars instead. If categories overlap or the total is unknown, establish the denominator or use a different pattern.

## Structure

State the whole and its unit. Draw segment lengths from actual proportions. Preserve small parts even if labels must sit outside. Label categories directly, avoiding a legend that depends on color alone. Keep the order meaningful and stable across repeated slides. Negative values and flows need another representation.

## Worked example

Fictional service launch. The sponsor wants to understand the planned allocation of a €200k launch budget. Categories are exclusive and cover the entire budget.

| Part | €k | Share |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Product | 100 | 50% |
| Operations | 50 | 25% |
| Research | 30 | 15% |
| Contingency | 20 | 10% |

**Before:** A bulleted list of the four amounts and the total.

**After headline:** “Product accounts for half of the launch budget”

**After layout:** Total above a 100% stacked bar. Four aligned labels below show names, amounts, and percentages. Use proportionate widths of 50, 25, 15, and 10 percent. Make clear that this is an allocation, not actual spending.

## Rationale and limits

Design judgment: one bounded bar makes the whole explicit. Common region is a Gestalt analogy for parts belonging to that whole. Segment length is the quantitative encoding; color only identifies categories. Do not suggest that the allocation is effective or optimal without evidence.

**Check:** Amounts sum to €200k and shares to 100%. An omitted category, rounded residual, or overlap must be disclosed rather than silently corrected.
# Golden cut

## Message

Give one already-supported message or visual field more room, with a smaller
field for evidence, context, or qualification. This is a compositional choice
about spatial emphasis. It is not a data relationship and does not replace the
pattern needed to compare values, show parts of a whole, represent membership,
dependencies, or change over time.

The conventional 61.8/38.2 split is an optional starting proportion for the
two fields. Adjust it to the content, screen, and reading order; equal or other
proportions may work better. No golden rectangle canvas, phi grid, recursive
subdivision, or particular typography is required.

## Useful when

- The audience has one clear primary takeaway and needs a smaller adjacent
  field for its evidence, source, caveat, or next step.
- A single image, quotation, or concise finding can lead while related context
  remains visible and clearly associated.

For example, a slide may give a service's supported launch date the main field
and put the conditions that qualify it beside the date. Keep the conditions
readable and label the primary statement so the spatial hierarchy does not
overstate certainty.

## Poor fit

- Two claims carry equal importance; making one field larger would imply an
  unsupported priority.
- A dense comparison needs shared row and column alignment for readers to
  compare the same criteria across options. Use a comparison pattern instead.
- The source has no clear primary message, or the medium leaves too little room
  for the secondary field to remain legible.

Adapt by changing the split, stacking fields for a narrow screen, simplifying
secondary material without dropping consequential caveats, or choosing another
pattern. If the primary message or the relative priority is unknown, ask what
the audience needs to decide and which claim has priority. If that cannot be
established, do not imply it with area.

## Keep quantitative meaning separate

The 61.8/38.2 spatial division does not mean that one quantity is 61.8% of a
whole. Do not use it to allocate data, compare unequal categories, encode a
winner, or imply aesthetic or cognitive superiority. Encode actual values with
their supported scales and attach labels to the evidence they describe. State
the source, scope, and uncertainty where they affect interpretation.

## Worked Lys example

The fictional brief is a supported launch date of 18 June, subject to a
security review due 10 June. The date is the main message; the review is a
qualification that must remain visible. The example uses a flexible main
column and a smaller context column rather than fixing the canvas to a ratio.

```html
<article data-notes="Fictional planning example. The date depends on the security review.">
  <h2>Launch is planned for 18 June</h2>
  <div class="launch-layout">
    <p class="launch-date"><time datetime="2027-06-18">18 June</time></p>
    <aside>
      <h3>Condition</h3>
      <p>Security review due 10 June. The launch date remains planned, not confirmed.</p>
    </aside>
  </div>
</article>
```

The date receives emphasis, while the condition remains explicit beside it.
This is a design judgment about the brief's stated priority, not a claim that
the split improves comprehension.
# Combining Kata with a renderer and a brand

The first iteration uses three separate responsibilities:

| Layer | Owns | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Kata | Message, relationships, encoding, reading order | A comparison matrix with common rows |
| Lys or another renderer | Output, navigation, sizing, notes, print behavior | Semantic HTML articles in a Lys deck |
| Optional brand skill | Visual identity | Approved typography and palette |

These are composition guidelines, not an adapter API. There is no separate `kata-lys` package yet.

## Lys

Read the available Lys instructions when producing a deck. The locally inspected Lys 1.2.1 contract uses a `div` with `data-lys` containing direct `article` children. Give each slide a heading and useful `data-notes`. Keep Kata's layout inside the article. Include the renderer CSS and JavaScript according to its packaging instructions, followed by the theme's CSS overrides. Use public `--lys-*` tokens; leave internal state attributes to Lys.

A dependency chart can be inline SVG with a title and text description. A comparison should remain a semantic table. Charts should retain their values in text or an accessible data table. A no-JavaScript view must still expose the slide content. Check both navigation and print output after integration.

The repository's `examples/kata-lys.html` is a working single-file specimen with ten slides: a before and after for each form. It bundles the locally available Lys build; it does not fetch a CDN resource at runtime. `examples/index.html` offers a scrollable comparison gallery. These are repository review artifacts and are not required when installing just this skill folder.

The specimen uses Lys's direct transition mode. A small chapter-navigation listener routes local anchor clicks through the public `Lys.Lys.from(element).goTo(id)` API so the active slide, accessibility state, and subsequent arrow-key navigation agree. Plain anchors remain usable without JavaScript. The vendored runtime itself is unchanged.

## Brand skills

If a Futurice brand skill is supplied, read and apply it. This repository does not contain or verify Futurice assets, tokens, or rules. Do not label a neutral example as on-brand. Keep the data's grouping and scales intact while changing the visual identity. If a brand palette cannot distinguish adjacent segments, retain explicit labels and boundaries instead of relying on hue.

## Example requests

“Use Kata to compare these three suppliers for the procurement team. Cost and delivery time matter, but don't choose a winner.”

“Use Kata with Lys to show how this budget is allocated. Apply the attached brand skill.”

“Improve this slide's structure using Kata. Preserve the supplied figures and explain the choice of pattern.”
# Time series

**Form:** A consistently defined measure observed at ordered times. **Audience question:** What changed, by how much, and when?

Use a line for a measure tracked across time, columns for discrete period totals, and a slope for a comparison of just two times. A process sequence without measurements belongs elsewhere. Do not join missing observations as though measured or space irregular dates equally.

## Structure

Put time on the horizontal axis and state the measure and unit on the vertical axis. Keep the definition and denominator consistent. Choose a visible scale that preserves the magnitude; bar charts need a zero baseline. A cropped line-chart axis can be useful, but label the bounds clearly. Mark forecasts and uncertainty distinctly from observations.

## Worked example

Fictional service pilot. The lead wants to understand average onboarding time for completed onboardings in January–June 2026. Each monthly cohort contains 20 completions, measured by the same rule. These are illustrative monthly means, not individual observations.

| Month | Jan | Feb | Mar | Apr | May | Jun |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Days | 10 | 9 | 7 | 8 | 6 | 5 |

**Before:** Six monthly numbers in a sentence.

**After headline:** “Average onboarding time fell from 10 to 5 days”

**After layout:** A line chart with a zero-to-12-day scale, equally spaced monthly points, direct values, and the April increase visible. Include cohort size and measurement scope. The headline describes endpoints without claiming a smooth decline.

## Rationale and limits

Design judgment: ordered position and a connected line expose the overall direction and the April reversal. Continuation is a Gestalt analogy. The line does not explain causality or prove statistical significance; variation within cohorts is unknown. Do not invent an intervention to explain the drop.

**Check:** Every point matches its month, April remains above March, and the 50% endpoint reduction uses January's 10 days as its baseline if quoted.
